Eight: Recoil{\*\bkmkend _Toc389883148}
\par }\pard \s15\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 
\par Axen's convoy disappeared into the Badlands as thoroughly as if it had never existed.  In the weeks after the raid in the Gulag, continued bad weather interfered with any search, and ongoing retaliatory strikes from Eden prevented their sending out any ve
hicles.
\par 
\par Shortly thereafter the colony was forced by the advance of the Blight to relocate, and with that disappeared any hope of finding Axen.  It was some comfort to Emma that Eden was probably having as much trouble locating him, and she had to hope that Axen w
ould be found when Axen wanted to be found.
\par 
\par As for the moment, Emma had more pressing concerns.  Plymouth's delay-plagued space program was finally about to bear fruit.  Work on a launch facility had begun before the relocation, and with the colony operati
onal on its new site, the launch gantry was already beginning to rise on the edge of the colony.  Soon work would begin on a launch vehicle, and a new satellite had to be ready for its test flight.
\par 
\par Emma's team had been assigned to develop the Blight detection package for the spacecraft, and to incorporate such intelligence gathering functions into the vehicle as space, weight, and power allowed.  This was in addition to their regular intelligence ga
thering functions of course.  Emma had never been more sure of her decision to keep a bunk in the lab, and several of the other scientists were soon doing the same.
\par 
\par The pressure had taken its toll on all of them.  They'd become testy and irritable.  Arguments over small problems and petty differences had become common, and thus Emma wasn't in the most receptive mood when Councilor Kozu arrived at the lab unannounced.

\par 
\par Emma was trying to get a balky Noesis computer to function while connected to the satellite's energy management system.  It wasn't going well, and she wasn't about to stop her work for any visitor.
\par 
\par "Elder," said Kozu, "I can see you're busy and I'm sorry to interrupt, but the colony leadership has a favor to ask of you."
\par 
\par Emma chuckled as she leaned over to check a wave form displayed on her ClipCom.  "Sure, in my copious free time." She gave him a look that made him flinch just a bit.  "We have a full-up test of the satellite's systems scheduled in three days, and I can't
 get this damned computer to talk to my instrument package, or sometimes to run at all."
\par 
\par "This satellite is certainly a colony priority, but we have something related to your intelligence activities, something I think nobody else in the colony is as well qualified to do."
\par 
\par She jiggled a power connector and checked the wave form again.  "More important than this satellite?"
\par 
\par He nodded.  "Just possibly.  Eden has contacted us and wishes to negotiate a treaty."
\par 
\par She stood bolt upright.  "What?"
\par 
\par "They say that an accident has damaged their human Gene Bank.  They want an agreement that protects our Gene Bank from damage in combat, and allows them to take custody if their starship is completed substantially before ours."
\par 
\par "Why would we agree to such a thing?"
\par 
\par Kozu looked apologetic.  "Frankly, your own intelligence reports show they are far ahead of us, and our projections of available resources and Blight spread don't paint a positive picture.  We may not make it.  The Eden leadership has suggested that they 
would make all possible space available on their starship for our children, and of course, saving the genetic heritage contained in the Gene Bank is our highest priority."
\par 
\par She shook her head in disbelief.  "We're giving up?"
\par 
\par "We're being realistic and keeping our options open."
\par 
\par She sighed.  "It sounds like you have this all worked out.  Why do you need me?"
\par 
\par "We want you to reach a settlement, yes, but the exact terms, of protecting the Gene Bank, of transfer of the Bank and the children, should it become necessary, all this needs to be worked out."
\par 
\par Emma's fists clinched on the workbench, the satellite forgotten.  Her mind flashed to other possibilities, desperate plans that she'd never dared voice before.  "We could steal }{\i\f5\cf4 their}{\f5\cf4  starship."
\par 
\par It was Kozu's turn to be shocked.
\par 
\par "Perhaps," she continued, "we could even send our Spiders in and reprogram their rockets on the pad, bring them over to our launch complex.  The ship in orbit will be defenseless, or close enough, especially if we take out their launch capability."
\par 
\par "You're suggesting total war with Eden.  If we go that far there would be no turning back.  It would be kill or be killed.  It's unthinkable!"
\par 
\par "I just thought of it, pup, and I can think of a lot more.  You've been understanding when I've told you I have certain intelligence resources in Eden that I cannot reveal.  I'm telling you now that information from those sources suggests that these peopl
e can't be trusted.  This is a trick of some kind."
\par 
\par He shook his head sadly.  "I'm afraid we have to have someth
ing more concrete than that.  I'm sorry, Elder.  If it's any consolation, I tend to agree with you, but the mood of the Council and the people doesn't support it.  They're tired of running, tired of fighting, tired of war." He looked away.  "I'll tell the
m to find someone else to negotiate the treaty."
\par 
\par She reached out and grabbed his forearm.  "No! I mean \emdash  when is the first negotiation session scheduled?"
\par 
\par "This evening.  It's short notice, but..."
\par 
\par "Go and tell them I'll do it, but I need more time.  Give me twenty-four hours."
\par 
\par "But Eden \emdash "
\par 
\par "Tell them we need more time to gain support for the proposal.  Tell them anything, just stall them.  If there are no new developments, I'll comply with your wishes, and get us the best treaty I can manage."
\par 
\par He seemed hesitant.
\par 
\par "Do it for me, pup.  Please.  Do it for us all."
\par 
\par He nodded.
\par 
\par She shooed him out of the room and looked back to the open storeroom where her bunk was kept, and at Frost sitting in the middle of the floor.  She walked in, closed the door, and sat down next to the computer.
\par 
\par "Frost, I need to talk to Panati in Eden."
\par 
\par Frost's icon appeared on its top panel.  "There has been no human contact initiated from Eden since our last relocation."
\par 
\par "Then initiate some.  Can you communicate with Kraft?"
\par 
\par "I can trace a link to Kraft through our normal channels, but such contact is a high-risk activity.  Kraft will not initiate contact without human instruction."
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 Would you? It's time to make use of this new assertiveness of yours.}{\f5\cf4   "Then your job is to persuade Kraft otherwise.  Do what has to be done, but convince Kraft that I must talk to Panati."
\par 
\par "This may take time."
\par 
\par "You've got an hour."
\par 
\par Frost said nothing, though the snowflake icon shifted to a pattern that indicated annoyance.
\par 
\par Emma sat down on her bunk and leaned back to wait.
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\qc\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 # # #
\par }\pard \s15\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 
\par Fifty-eight minutes later, Brook Panati appeared on Emma's EnterCom screen.  He looked out of brea
th, and for once wasn't wearing a spacesuit, though he was in a vehicle cab.  The vehicle didn't appear to be moving.  "You could have done something more subtle than having Kraft send a Repair Vehicle to tap on my window."
\par 
\par "I only set the timeline, not the method of contact.  I apologize, but I have an emergency, and you owe me a favor."
\par 
\par "For killing Axen?" He frowned.  "I'm sorry, it probably wasn't your fault."
\par 
\par "Axen isn't dead."
\par 
\par His frown turned into a big smile, and he leaned closer to the camera.  "Is he there? They told us he was killed."
\par 
\par "He isn't here.  Actually, I don't know how much I should tell you."
\par 
\par Panati nodded.  "You're right.  Don't tell me anything.  I've been followed lately.  They suspect me.  That's why I haven't contacted you.  I'm taking a huge risk right now, breaking into a Garage to find a vehicle."  He wrinkled his nose.  "It's complica
ted and irrelevant.  We may not have much time.  What can I do for you?"
\par 
\par "Eden wants to negotiate a treaty to gain access to our Gene Bank.  They say yours was damaged in an accident, and if their ship is ready first, they want to take it, along with our children, with them."
\par 
\par Panati looked shocked, and outraged.  "There was no fragging accident.  The Masters }{\i\f5\cf4 destroyed}{\f5\cf4 
 our human Gene Bank, and it was intentional.  They believe they can create a master race without it.  Though they're in control, there's a strong resistance movement.  If they can destroy your Gene Bank, they'll make any debate useless.  You can't let th
em near it."
\par 
\par "I don't want to, but our leaders are caving in.  I need something to take to them, some hard evidence, and I don't have much time."
\par 
\par He looked at the camera blankly.  "I don't know what I can give you.  I don't suppose they'd take my word for it?"
\par 
\par She shook her head.  "I don't know if I can even reveal your existence without destroying my own credibility, and even if I could, they'd have no special reason to trust you.  I need proof of their hostile intentions."
\par 
\par He thought for a minute.  "I just don't know of anything.  As I said, I've had to keep a low profile recently.  I'm out of the loop."
\par 
\par She considered the situation.  It seemed hopeless.  Then an idea struck her.  She'd never mentioned her other contact in Eden to Panati, in part because she didn't know whom she could trust, and in part because keeping them separate offered some redundanc
y.  If one source was caught or cut off, the other might continue to feed information.  }{\i\f5\cf4 But desperate times call for...  }{\f5\cf4 "I have another contact in Eden, someone who has provide
d us useful information through another channel.  I don't know if you can trust him, but he may have information we can use, and I have no other way of contacting him." 
\par 
\par Panati looked surprised, but interested.
\par 
\par "Do you know a scientist named Eldon Jensen?"
\par 
\par Panati nodded.  "He's been a somewhat reluctant information source in the past." He smiled and chuckled.  "Didn't know Eldon had it in him, a spy at heart after all."
\par 
\par "As I said, I don't know if you can trust him."
\par 
\par "My gut says I can.  I'm willing to risk it, anyway."
\par 
\par "Good.  I have less than twenty-four hours."
\par 
\par "I'll get back to you one way or the other."  He blinked and cleared his throat.  "Unless of course, I don't."
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\qc\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 # # #
